pact they're not  contiguous they don't give a sense of  any kind of community which is what  representation is all about the United  States is the only advanced democracy in  the world which allows politicians to  actually draw their district lines  so our redline if you will we say  there's no silver bullet and how you  create the Commission's to do this but  we just feel that the politician  shouldn't be directly involved in that  process it's completely self-serving and  we get the kind of districts we do  because of that that would bring us to  California with prop 11 and with the two  competing propositions that are coming  up in the current election prop 11  established a 14 person Commission to  oversee the redistricting of state  legislative districts correct the  purpose was to draw lines for the state  assembly the state Senate and the State  Board of Equalization all those lines  would now not be drawn in the  legislature by members of the  legislature they would be drawn by an  independent commission which was  selected to ensure a very non partisan  makeup this time around the proponents  of reform have tried to  extend exactly that proposition 11  Commission to congressional districts  one of the reasons why proposition 11  didn't face the kind of opposition that  we've had in the past is that it  excluded congressional district so  proposition 20 would in effect finish  the job but proponents of reform have  two jobs to do now in this election one  is to pass proposition 20 to extend it  to congressional districts but the other  is to win proposition 27 which would  rescind in effect proposition 11 and  roll back to a time when the legislators  are in control of everything  speaking of that we should probably talk  about the Texas example where the  Democrats famously lit out for the  border and stayed in a hotel and on the  border with Oklahoma three Democrats  from the Texas House of Representatives  bolted for the Oklahoma State Line  yesterday to prevent a vote which would  redraw voting districts that would favor  the Republican House Majority the delay  redistricting was a classic partisan  redistricting and you know it was a  power play the Republicans had control  and they decided they were going to  redraw the map to ensure that they got  more Republican congressional seats and  they did it they did it successfully  there was a lot of drama in it you had  you know it was really surreal when you  think of American democracy in one  breath and at the same time think about  bounty hunters chasing democratically  elected state legislators across state  lines who are holed up in a Holiday Inn  it's not exactly the first you know  conception of American democracy but but  I think those types of gerrymanders are  actually as as in the face as they are  there are less dangerous to the system  if you will than what I call a  bipartisan gerrymander because in a in a  partisan gerrymander there's always  another side to scream holy hell and and  people find out about and they  understand it's a power play in  California they actually pass  the 2001 gerrymander the day after 9/11  Zara Prada mr. president members this is  the Senate and congressional  redistricting for the decade bill I am  proud and happy to report that it has  bipartisan support and it was a  completely closed-door session between  Democrats and Republicans getting  together behind closed doors and the  public never heard about it and the  press for obvious reasons paid very  little attention to it because of the  timing of when they did that those are  the types of invisible gerrymanders that  I think really eat away at our system  because they disenfranchise people all  for incumbency protection that's the  goal it's not to the Democrats weren't  trying to get more seats the Republicans  weren't trying to get more seats  everybody just wanted to freeze the map  the way it was and you're seeing that in  both 2008 in the 2010 election which is  